<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The interaction contracts, as something a customer can reference.

    They lived in Boltway.Interaction.Tests, which was already `IsPackable=true` with the right
    reason written on it — "a contract nobody outside this repository can run is a contract only this
    repository is held to." What it produced was not usable. Measured on the packed output:

      * dependencies included Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk, xunit.runner.visualstudio and
        coverlet.collector, so referencing the contract dragged a test *runner* and a coverage
        collector into the customer's project;
      * the assembly shipped a runtimeconfig.json, because it was packaged as an executable test
        project rather than as a library;
      * and it carried this repository's own concrete tests — DefaultInteractionLayoutTests, the
        themed-renderer suite, and the sabotage suite with its deliberately broken renderer. A
        customer running `dotnet test` would have run Boltway's tests inside their own suite
        and read the results as being about their renderer.

    So: the abstract contracts and the small markup reader live here, with xunit and nothing else.
    The derivations and the sabotage suite stay next door, where they are this repository's tests
    rather than anybody's contract.

    No test runner here on purpose. This assembly is never executed by itself — a customer's own
    test project supplies the runner, and adding one would put a second copy in their build.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <IsTestProject>false</IsTestProject>
    <Description>
      The IInteractionRenderer and IInteractionLayout contracts, as xunit base classes. Derive from
      them, supply your implementation, and the suite that defines the seam runs against it.
    </Description>

    <!--
      CA1707 (no underscores in a public member name) off, CS1591 (missing XML comment) left on,
      and the split is the point.

      The names are underscored because a contract method's name is what a customer reads in their
      own test runner when it fails: `Consent_shows_the_host_of_the_client_id` is the finding.
      Renaming it to satisfy a convention meant for API surfaces would cost the one place the
      message has to land.

      The docs stay required because this *is* an API surface now. A customer deriving from these
      types sees the summaries in IntelliSense, and "what is this assertion for" is exactly the
      question the seam exists to answer. `tests/Directory.Build.props` turns both off, for
      tests that nobody outside this repository reads; only one of its two reasons applies here.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1707</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The assertion library only. Not Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk, not the runner, not coverlet:
         those belong to whatever project executes tests, which is never this one. -->
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Using Include="Xunit" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The shipped assembly only, and deliberately no InternalsVisibleTo grant: this project is
    compiled against exactly what a customer has. If a contract ever needs an internal type to
    express an assertion, that type belongs on the public surface instead — the customer writing the
    renderer needs it just as much as the contract does.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Boltway.AuthorizationServer\Boltway.AuthorizationServer.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
